WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.791
Consistency becomes easier to discuss when we
describe an actual user's sequence of

00:00:04.791 --> 00:00:10.101
operations. Monotonic reads means that after a
session observes a version, a later read does

00:00:10.101 --> 00:00:12.179
not go backward to an older version.

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:17.046
Read-your-writes means that after the session
completes an update, its later reads include

00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:21.698
that update or a later one. These promises are
different. A user who has never written

00:00:21.698 --> 00:00:26.403
anything can still suffer a nonmonotonic read
when requests alternate between a current

00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:27.809
replica and a lagging one.

00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:32.713
A user who saves a profile can fail to read
their own write even if every subsequent read

00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:35.027
consistently returns the same old version.

00:00:35.027 --> 00:00:40.047
Draw two read arrows from one client: the first
reaches A at version eight, and the second

00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:45.179
reaches B at version seven. That is a monotonic-
read violation. Then replace the first arrow

00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:50.255
with a completed write of version eight: the
same stale second response violates read-your-

00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:50.645
writes.

00:00:50.645 --> 00:00:55.606
Routing the session to one sufficiently current
node can help, but the promise must survive

00:00:55.606 --> 00:01:00.403
reconnection and failover. A session can carry a
minimum version or replication position

00:01:00.403 --> 00:01:05.309
that a new node must satisfy. Waiting until that
position is applied preserves the promise

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:10.160
at the cost of delay; accepting an older
response preserves immediate availability at the

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.251
cost of the promise.

00:01:11.251 --> 00:01:13.492
The system needs to choose deliberately.

00:01:13.492 --> 00:01:18.599
A quorum is a threshold of replica responses
required before an operation is treated as

00:01:18.599 --> 00:01:23.940
complete. With three replicas, consider writes
acknowledged by two and reads answered using

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:29.282
two. If A and B acknowledged a completed write,
any set of two read responders must contain

00:01:29.282 --> 00:01:29.693
A or B.

00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:34.749
The overlap is the essential arithmetic: R plus
W greater than N guarantees intersection

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:39.576
between the selected read set and the
acknowledged write set, under a stable replica

00:01:39.576 --> 00:01:43.426
membership. It does not mean the two read values
must be identical.

00:01:43.426 --> 00:01:48.687
A read can receive one older value and one newer
value, so the system still needs meaningful

00:01:48.687 --> 00:01:51.317
version information and a reconciliation rule.

00:01:51.426 --> 00:01:56.435
Change the thresholds to see the workload
tradeoff. With W equal to three and R equal to

00:01:56.435 --> 00:02:01.673
one, a completed write has reached every
replica, allowing a subsequent read to use a
single

00:02:01.673 --> 00:02:03.893
response under the model's assumptions.

00:02:03.926 --> 00:02:09.194
But the write must wait for the slowest required
participant, and one unavailable replica

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:14.640
prevents completion. With W and R both equal to
two, one unavailable replica can still leave

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.363
enough responders for both kinds of operation.

00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:22.509
This is a latency and failure-policy choice, not
just a consistency switch. The relevant

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:27.477
replicas belong to this key; another key may
have a different replica group and remain

00:02:27.477 --> 00:02:29.673
unaffected by the same failed machine.

00:02:29.673 --> 00:02:34.647
Begin with an observable history rather than a
product label. A user completes an update to

00:02:34.647 --> 00:02:39.677
a shared register and then another user starts a
read. Linearizability requires that read to

00:02:39.677 --> 00:02:44.652
behave consistently with a single order in which
each operation takes effect at one instant

00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:46.620
between its invocation and response.

00:02:46.673 --> 00:02:51.430
Real-time precedence matters: an operation
completed before another began must appear

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:56.187
earlier in that order. Operations that overlap
may admit more than one order. This is

00:02:56.187 --> 00:03:01.281
stronger than merely preserving each client's
own sequence. A diagram should therefore show

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:06.430
request and response intervals, not just a list
of values with no indication of when clients

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.445
were told an operation had finished.

00:03:08.673 --> 00:03:13.724
Compare that promise with a replicated social
display. One session may always read its own

00:03:13.724 --> 00:03:18.832
updates and never move backward, while another
session still sees a previous version. Those

00:03:18.832 --> 00:03:23.547
session guarantees can make the interface
understandable without creating a globally

00:03:23.547 --> 00:03:24.669
linearizable object.

00:03:24.673 --> 00:03:29.899
Conversely, a linearizable single-key operation
does not automatically make a transaction

00:03:29.899 --> 00:03:35.185
spanning several keys serializable. The design
question is which unit of state needs which

00:03:35.185 --> 00:03:40.470
guarantee. For a shared reservation count,
ordering updates may be essential; for a cached

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.933
display of recent activity, bounded or eventual
freshness may be acceptable.

00:03:45.173 --> 00:03:49.974
Naming the guarantee prevents students from
treating every successful quorum read as proof

00:03:49.974 --> 00:03:51.681
of the strongest possible model.
